      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the expression \\(\\frac{(N-1)!(N)}{(N+1)!}\\), we will start by simplifying the factorials in the numerator and the denominator.\n\nFirst, recall that \\((N+1)! = (N+1) \\cdot N \\cdot (N-1)!\\). Therefore, we can rewrite the denominator \\((N+1)!\\) as:\n\\[\n(N+1)! = (N+1) \\cdot N \\cdot (N-1)!\n\\]\nSubstituting this into the original expression, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{(N-1)!(N)}{(N+1)!} = \\frac{(N-1)!(N)}{(N+1) \\cdot N \\cdot (N-1)!}\n\\]\nNext, we can cancel out the \\((N-1)!\\) in the numerator and the denominator:\n\\[\n\\frac{(N-1)!(N)}{(N+1) \\cdot N \\cdot (N-1)!} = \\frac{N}{(N+1) \\cdot N}\n\\]\nThe \\(N\\) in the numerator and the denominator cancel each other out:\n\\[\n\\frac{N}{(N+1) \\cdot N} = \\frac{1}{N+1}\n\\]\nThus, the simplified form of the expression is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{N+1}}\n\\]",
